{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785092248,"nonce":"HIUa9tBHDFOS1Di6gRkPtlg0Vu0=","title":"HIGH: a truncated workspace drain tar publishes a PARTIAL tree as the step's authoritative snapshot","message":"Silent data loss. The `exec` drain has no error channel, so a `tar` that dies\npartway produces a *valid-looking* smaller workspace snapshot, and the engine\nrecords it as the Step's evidence.\n\nVerified by reading code at `bd30a71`. Not part of any ADR-0061 slice — filed\non its own merits.\n\n---\n\n\n`crates/scarab-executor-k8s/src/lib.rs:801` `exec_capture_stdout`:\n\n- `:812` sets `.stderr(false)`, so the remote command's stderr is discarded.\n- `:824` `proc.join().await` is the only failure check, and it never calls\n  `take_status()`.\n\nIn kube-4, `AttachedProcess::join()` returns `Err` **only** for websocket or\ntask failures. The remote command's exit `Status` frame goes to a *separate*\n`status_rx` channel and the read loop breaks `Ok(())`\n(`kube-client-4.0.0/src/api/remote_command.rs:347-352`). So the exit code of\n`tar` is simply never consulted.\n\nConsequence at the drain site (`:505`,\n`tar -cf - -C /workspace .` in the egress container):\n\n1. `tar` dies partway (OOM, node pressure, container kill, websocket reset).\n2. `exec_capture_stdout` returns the **truncated bytes as `Ok`**.\n3. `unpack_dir` only fails if the truncation lands mid-header. Truncation at\n   a 512-byte record boundary unpacks **cleanly** into a *partial tree*.\n4. That partial tree is `ingest`ed and written as the step's authoritative\n   `scarab.io/workspace-root`.\n\nDownstream steps then consume a workspace that is missing files, and the\ndurable record asserts that snapshot IS what the Step produced. This is the\none place where the tunnel's \"unresumable\" property upgrades from *slow* to\n**wrong** — and it is exactly the class `CONTEXT.md` §2 forbids: a claim the\nrecord cannot back.\n\nThe same blindness applies to `exec_with_stdin` (`:764`, `let _ =\nproc.join().await` at `:796`). There it is *mostly* benign — the comment\nexplains the marker-file teardown race and the feed is retried from the Pod\nstate — but the blanket `let _ =` also swallows real feed failures.\n\n\n`crates/scarab-executor-k8s/tests/cluster.rs:37-48` explicitly tolerates\ntransient `workspace: broken pipe` at roughly 1-in-9 on CI kind (git-bug\na0b42ad). So the failure mode this depends on is *observed*, not theoretical\n— the test suite has been papering over it. The observed variant surfaces as\nan error; the dangerous variant is the one that does not.\n\n\n**ADR-0061 s3-drain deletes this code path entirely** (the drain becomes a\nScarab-owned helper pushing hashed blobs straight to the workspace service,\nwhere a hash mismatch is rejected at the door, so a partial push cannot\nproduce a valid root). If s3-drain lands and is live-verified, this is moot\nand the ticket closes with that as its evidence.\n\n**Until then it is a live silent-data-loss path** and deserves a defensive\nfix, which is cheap:\n\n- Call `take_status()` before/alongside `join()` and treat a non-zero exit —\n  or an absent status frame — as an error; and/or\n- keep `.stderr(true)` and surface it in the error message so the *cause* is\n  legible; and/or\n- frame the tar with a trailing sentinel the server verifies (tar's own\n  two-zero-block end-of-archive marker is already a sentinel — check for it\n  before `unpack_dir` rather than after).\n\nAny of the three converts silent partial evidence into a `DriveErr::Transient`\nretry, which is squarely inside the at-least-once contract.\n\n\n- A drain whose `tar` exits non-zero, or whose stream is truncated at a\n  record boundary, results in a **retry**, never a published snapshot.\n- `crates/scarab-executor-k8s/tests/cluster.rs` stops tolerating\n  `workspace: broken pipe` as an expected outcome (or the tolerance is\n  narrowed to the *reported*-error case only).","files":null}]}